// BUGS #98(c) — bindings on an imported enum must get their declared types.
//
// `enum_fields` is keyed by the enum's declared (unqualified) name, but a
// subject's inferred type is the qualified spelling the importer sees. So
// get_enum_binding_type looked up "AST.Stmt.VarDecl", missed, and returned Any
// for every binding of every arm — a match on any imported enum was effectively
// unchecked. check_exhaustiveness had open-coded the prefix-stripping fallback,
// so non-exhaustive matches on AST.Stmt *were* reported while nothing else about
// them was, which made the checker look like it worked.
//
// Both arms return String here on purpose: with one arm String and one Int the
// result unifies to `String|Int` and the assignment to Int is accepted for a
// different and legitimate reason, which would have made this test pass
// vacuously.

import "@ast" as AST

var s: AST.Stmt = AST.Stmt.VarDecl("x", "Int", AST.Expr.NilLit, "")

fun fallback(): String { return "0" }

// VarDecl's first field is declared String. Assigning it to Int must be an error.
var bad: Int = match (s) {
    VarDecl(name, type_ann, initializer, docstring) => name
    _ => fallback()
}

IO.println(bad)
